{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.LadderConventionLocalisation","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LadderConventionLocalisation","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.QBetaCategoricalPrecisionLadder"],"declarations":17,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.N2_factor_eq_bracketRatio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.N2_factor_eq_bracketRatio","kind":"theorem","line":216,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L216-L222","doc":"**The `N=2` rung is a function of `q` alone in the D frame** — no free\nparameter. `N2_factor = 1 − ([3]_q/[2]_q²)·ℏ_q⁴`, with the coupling supplied\nby the forced D weights rather than assumed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.hoefsmitB_sq_eq_bracketThree_div_bracketTwo_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.hoefsmitB_sq_eq_bracketThree_div_bracketTwo_sq","kind":"theorem","line":202,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L202-L214","doc":"**`b² = [3]_q / [2]_q²`** — the Hoefsmit coupling squared IS a quantum-integer\nratio in the D frame.\n\nImmediate from `hoefsmitB_sq_eq_one_sub_stdWeightD` (`b² = 1 − y_(2,1)^D`)\nand `stdWeightD_eq_jones` (`y_(2,1)^D = 1/[2]_q²`), using\n`bracketTwo_sq_sub_bracketThree_eq_one`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.bracketTwo_sq_sub_bracketThree_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.bracketTwo_sq_sub_bracketThree_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":194,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L194-L200","doc":"**`[2]_q² − [3]_q = 1`** — the quantum-integer identity behind the collapse\nbelow. With `[2]_q = q + q⁻¹` and `[3]_q = q² + 1 + q⁻²`:\n`(q + q⁻¹)² = q² + 2 + q⁻²`, so subtracting `[3]_q` leaves exactly `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.dWeights_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.dWeights_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":159,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L159-L167","doc":"The D-weights and coupling at the classical point `q = 1`:\n`y_(2,1)^D = ¼`, `y_(3)^D = ½`, `b² = ¾`. (`c₃`'s value at `q=1`,\n`J_Hopf(1) = −2`, is a **topological input** — the Jones polynomial of the\nHopf link — NOT derivable from these weights; there is no forced-`c₃` lemma.\nThe provable D-structure is the weight/coupling algebra above.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.dWeights_markov_sigmasigma","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.dWeights_markov_sigmasigma","kind":"theorem","line":150,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L150-L157","doc":"**The S2 D-weights solve the σσ Markov equation at `z_D`:**\n`q²·y_(3)^D − y_(2,1)^D = z_D²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.dWeights_markov_sigma","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.dWeights_markov_sigma","kind":"theorem","line":140,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L140-L148","doc":"**The S2 D-weights solve the σ Markov equation at `z_D = q³/(q²+1)`:**\n`q·y_(3)^D + (q−q⁻¹)·y_(2,1)^D = z_D` (with `y_(1³)^D = 0`). Formalises the\nS2 symbolic solution (`gram-radical-exact`) in Lean. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.stdWeightD_eq_jones","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.stdWeightD_eq_jones","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L131-L138","doc":"**`y_(2,1)^D = 1/[2]_q²`** — the standard-block weight is the Jones-closure\nvalue, matching the `(q+q⁻¹)²·y₂₁ = 1` conclusion of\n`QOU.BraidsAndKnots.markovWeights_eq_jones_of_threeRow_eq_zero`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.hoefsmitB_sq_eq_trivWeightD_add_stdWeightD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.hoefsmitB_sq_eq_trivWeightD_add_stdWeightD","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L125-L129","doc":"**`b² = y_(3)^D + y_(2,1)^D`** — complementary form of\n`hoefsmitB_sq_eq_one_sub_stdWeightD`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.trivWeightD_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.trivWeightD_eq","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L121-L123","doc":"`y_(3)^D = 1 − 2·y_(2,1)^D` (two-row relation). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.dWeights_normalised","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.dWeights_normalised","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L113-L119","doc":"**D-weight normalisation** `y_(3)^D + 2·y_(2,1)^D = 1` (with `y_(1³)^D = 0`):\nthe S2 weight vector is a normalised trace on `H₃(q)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.trivWeightD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.trivWeightD","kind":"def","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L110-L111","doc":"D-frame trivial-block `(3)` Wedderburn weight (S2): `y_(3)^D = (q⁴+1)/(q²+1)²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.hoefsmitB_sq_eq_one_sub_stdWeightD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.hoefsmitB_sq_eq_one_sub_stdWeightD","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L91-L108","doc":"**The Hoefsmit coupling squared is the complement of the D standard-block\nweight: `b² = 1 − y_(2,1)^D`.**\n\nThis is the D-frame structural anchor of the whole `N2..N5` tail: the\ncoupling `b` that generates the Kashiwara/Hopf rungs is exactly\n`√(1 − y_(2,1)^D)`. The identity is **convention-D specific** — under C the\n`H₃(q)` weights carry `√q` and keep `y_(1³) ≠ 0`, and `b² ≠ 1 − y_(2,1)^C`\n(`hoefsmit_b_vs_d_weights_probe.py`). So `b`, hence `N2..N5`, is naturally a\nD-frame object, a positive structural reason the categorical tails are\ncorrectly framed in D (bean `qou-cds1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.stdWeightD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.stdWeightD","kind":"def","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L85-L89","doc":"D-frame standard-block `(2,1)` Wedderburn weight of `H₃(q)` (S2 PR #5185,\n`gram-radical-exact`): `y_(2,1)^D = q²/(q²+1)²`. At `z_D = q³/(q²+1)` the\nthree Markov equations force `y_(1³) = 0` (two-row Temperley–Lieb) and pin\nthis value. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Q_beta_categorical_ofPhase_tail_common","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Q_beta_categorical_ofPhase_tail_common","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L65-L75","doc":"**The N=2..5 tail is phase-independent**, hence Markov-convention-blind:\ntwo phases `φ₁`, `φ₂` yield ladders differing only by their `cos` factors,\nwith `ckTail` — and so every categorical `c_k`, `k ≥ 2` — common. Stated in\nthe division-free cross-multiplied form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Q_beta_categorical_eq_ofPhase","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Q_beta_categorical_eq_ofPhase","kind":"theorem","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L56-L63","doc":"**The Markov-parameter convention enters the categorical ladder only through\nthe N=1 eigenphase.** `Q_beta_categorical` is exactly the `φ = alpha q`\ninstance of `Q_beta_categorical_ofPhase`, whose `ckTail` (rungs N=2..5,\ncarrying `c₂ = −1`, `c₃ = −2`, `c₄`, `c₅`) is a factor independent of `φ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Q_beta_categorical_ofPhase","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Q_beta_categorical_ofPhase","kind":"def","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L49-L54","doc":"The categorical ladder with the single-wrap eigenphase supplied as an\nexplicit parameter `φ`. The C-frame instance is `φ = alpha q`; convention D\nsupplies the mirror-covariant `alpha_D − h²/4`. Every other input is\nconvention-blind. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.ckTail","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.ckTail","kind":"def","line":43,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LadderConventionLocalisation.lean#L43-L47","doc":"The N=2..5 tail of the categorical ladder — the product of the rungs whose\ncoefficients are the `c_k` (`k ≥ 2`). Each factor is a closed form in `q`\nthrough `hoefsmitB` and `hbarQ'` alone; none reads the Markov parameter. ","signatureChunk":0}]}